//! What a component's own function body does with its props, read from the syntax alone (trail 405): which props it
//! reads, the JSX elements it writes with their attributes in source order, and whether it hands its props object on
//! whole. Facts only: what they mean for which props are relevant is the resolver's to decide, and nothing here is
//! emitted.
//!
//! The function is the one a mapping's declaration holds, through the wrapper calls the extractor unwraps (`memo`,
//! `forwardRef`, a factory's first argument). Elements built with `createElement` are not recorded.
//!
//! # Conservative by construction
//!
//! Names are resolved by `oxc_semantic`, which binds every reference to its symbol: a parameter, `var`, `let`,
//! function or class name, catch or loop binding of the same name is another symbol, and what is the component's is
//! decided by the symbol. Every reference to the props object (the first parameter and each rest binding taken from
//! it) is classified by the node around it, through TypeScript's `as`, `satisfies`, `!` and `<T>`; only three are
//! safe:
//!
//! - the object of a member read with a static or string-literal key (`props.x`, `props?.['x']`) that is not called
//!   (`props.f()` passes the object as `this`), written, deleted or destructured into: `x` is read;
//! - the initializer of an object destructuring (`const { a, b = 1, ...r } = props`) or the right side of a
//!   destructuring assignment that is a statement of its own (its value is the props object): its static keys are read, and a rest binding is the props object again;
//! - a JSX spread attribute (`{...props}`): recorded as a spread, and the whole props object spread is handed on.
//!
//! Any other reference (a call argument, `new`, an attribute value, an object or array literal, a `return`, a
//! computed key, an assignment, `typeof`, a type query) hands the object on, so `reads` is complete only when
//! `handed_on` is false.
//!
//! # Invariants
//!
//! - Unknown unless proven: a component with no function body to read (`declare function`, an overload signature,
//!   `declare const`) has no facts at all; `reads` is complete only while `handed_on` is false. The props object
//!   named by no parameter (`arguments`, a rest parameter) hands it on.
//! - The contexts that only read a member are an allow-list ([`Reader::is_read`]); a parent node kind not named there
//!   hands the object on, so a form the parser adds later is unknown, not a silent read.
//! - By binding, never by spelling: references resolve through `oxc_semantic` symbols.
//! - A props binding that is ever written or redeclared is unknown: the body hands it on and records nothing through it.
//! - Every read is recorded: an attribute's prop is named for each form of the member (`props.x`, `props['x']`,
//!   `props?.x`, `props?.['x']`, `props!.x`) by the same rule that records the read.
//! - One owner: an element belongs to the function node it is written in.
//!
//! A function or class the body binds and renders as a JSX tag, by symbol (`const Item = () => <li/>; return
//! <Item/>`), is a component of its own: its references to the props object still count, but its elements are the
//! file's ([`JsxUsage`]), not the parent's.
//!
//! # Owned by node and ordered by position
//!
//! An element belongs to the exact function or class node it is written in, never to the extent of a declaration or
//! wrapper call around it: JSX in a wrapper's other arguments (`withOptions(fn, { fallback: <Icon /> })`) is written
//! where the wrapper is called. Reads and rest bindings are ordered by source position, not by the order references
//! are classified in (symbol by symbol).

use std::collections::VecDeque;

use oxc_ast::AstKind;
use oxc_ast::ast::*;
use oxc_ast_visit::{Visit, walk};
use oxc_semantic::{AstNodes, Scoping, SemanticBuilder};
use oxc_span::GetSpan;
use oxc_syntax::node::NodeId;
use oxc_syntax::scope::ScopeFlags;
use oxc_syntax::symbol::SymbolId;
    pub(crate) body: Option<BodyFacts>,
    /// For a component a factory call builds, the props the call's own arguments give it (`extractor::factory_props`):
    /// the annotated props type of the render function it is given, or the variant keys of the style recipe it is
    /// given. The resolver adds those its props type lacks.
    pub(crate) factory_props: Option<CollectedType>,
    /// The type parameters of that render function (`<T,>(props: { v: T }) => ...`). They are in scope for
    /// `factory_props` only, never for the callee's result type, where the same name may be a module-level declaration.
    pub(crate) factory_type_params: Vec<TypeName>,
}

/// What a component's function body does with its props, read from the syntax alone (`extractor::body_facts`). Facts
/// only: no judgement of relevance or forwarding is made where they are collected.
#[derive(Debug, Clone, Default, PartialEq, Eq, Serialize, Deserialize)]
pub(crate) struct BodyFacts {
    /// The props the function reads, in first-read order: the keys destructured from the props object (in the
    /// parameter or the body), and the keys of member reads on it or a rest binding (`props.x`, `props?.['x']`,
    /// `rest.x`). Complete only when `handed_on` is false: a handed-on object can be read anywhere.
    pub(crate) reads: Vec<std::string::String>,
    /// Every rest binding destructured from the props object or from another rest binding (`...rest`), in the order
    /// they are reached.
    pub(crate) rests: Vec<std::string::String>,
    /// Every JSX element the body writes, in source order, nested components' excepted.
    pub(crate) elements: Vec<JsxElementFact>,
    /// Whether the props object or a rest binding is used in any way other than a member read with a literal key, a
    /// destructuring, or a JSX spread: passed to a call, stored, returned and so on (`extractor::body_facts`). When it
    /// is, `reads` is a lower bound. A JSX spread of either is an element's attribute, not an escape.
    pub(crate) handed_on: bool,
}

/// One JSX element a component body writes.
#[derive(Debug, Clone, PartialEq, Eq, Serialize, Deserialize)]
pub(crate) struct JsxElementFact {
    /// The element's tag.
    pub(crate) tag: JsxTag,
    /// Its attributes in source order, so a named attribute's place before or after a spread is kept.
    pub(crate) attributes: Vec<JsxAttributeFact>,
}

/// A JSX tag, by the JSX rule: a lowercase first letter, a dash or a namespace makes it intrinsic.
#[derive(Debug, Clone, PartialEq, Eq, Serialize, Deserialize)]
pub(crate) enum JsxTag {
    /// A host element: `div`, `my-element`, `svg:path`.
    Intrinsic(std::string::String),
    /// A component named by an identifier: `Button`.
    Identifier(std::string::String),
    /// A component reached through a member path: `chakra.button`.

